---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { EN } from "../../data/copy";

const dev = `npm ci                    # install from the lockfile
npm run tauri -- dev      # the app, with the Vite server on 127.0.0.1:1420
npm run dev               # Vite only, if you open the renderer in a browser
npm run sidecar           # rebuild the MCP bridge (debug)`;

const checks = `npm run typecheck
npm run lint
npm test

cargo fmt --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml -- --check
cargo test --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml --lib
cargo build --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml --no-default-features`;

const release = `npm run sidecar:release
npm run tauri -- build --bundles dmg`;
---

<Docs
  copy={EN}
  title="Build and run"
  description="Prerequisites, development commands, the checks to run before shipping, and how to rebuild the MCP bridge."
  slug="build"
>
  <p class="docs__lead">
    Everything ships from source. There is no binary, no installer and no update channel today, so
    this is also the installation path.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>macOS, Apple silicon or Intel.</li>
    <li>Node.js 22.18 or newer, with npm.</li>
    <li>A Rust toolchain, installed with rustup.</li>
    <li>The Xcode command line tools.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Development</h2>
  <pre><code>{dev}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>npm run tauri -- dev</code> is the normal loop: it starts Vite on port 1420 and opens the Tauri window.</li>
    <li>A Rust change triggers a rebuild and a window restart; the workspace is restored on the next launch.</li>
    <li><code>npm run sidecar</code> is only needed when the bridge, the protocol or the tool list changes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Checks before shipping</h2>
  <pre><code>{checks}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>npm test</code> runs with <code>node --experimental-strip-types</code>. Avoid enums,
    parameter properties and decorators in any file imported by the tests: they are not
    stripped.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Node tests cover the workspace, the registry, extensions, the brief, the data tiles,
    OpenAlex, versions and Store navigation. The Rust tests cover storage, packages (folder, zip,
    checksum, source), the terminal, agents, the explorer, PDF and migrations.
  </p>

  <h2>After an MCP change</h2>
  <p>
    If <code>src-tauri/src/bin/spark-mcp.rs</code>, the protocol or the tool list changes, rebuild
    the bridge so clients get the new binary:
  </p>
  <pre><code>npm run sidecar</code></pre>
  <p>
    A client that is already connected keeps its cached tool list and may need a new session to
    see a new core tool.
  </p>

  <h2>Release</h2>
  <pre><code>{release}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The DMG is unsigned and not notarised. macOS will ask you to confirm on first open, and there
    is no update channel: a new version means a new build.
  </p>

  <h2>Formatting</h2>
  <pre><code>npm run format
cargo fmt --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml</code></pre>

  <h2>Changing a contract</h2>
  <p>
    The code wins, but a contract change (SDK, manifest, registry index, Tauri command, storage
    format) updates the matching document in <code>doc/</code> in the same change. A structural
    decision gets an entry in <code>doc/decisions/</code>.
  </p>
</Docs>
